{"name":"Big Horn Mountains","hex":"#b79e94","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"big","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Biangai."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of great size, large.","Fat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/big"},{"term":"horn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard growth of keratin that protrudes from the top of the head of certain animals, usually paired.","Any similar real or imaginary growth or projection such as the elongated tusk of a narwhal, the eyestalk of a snail, the pointed growth on the nose of a rhinoceros, or the hornlike projection on the head of a demon or similar."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of an animal, to assault with the horns.","To furnish with horns."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horn"},{"term":"mountains","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of mountain","mountain range"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountains"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bighorn Mountains","blocks":[{"text":"The Bighorn Mountains are a mountain range in northern Wyoming and southern Montana in the United States, forming a northwest-trending spur from the Rocky Mountains extending approximately 200 mi (320 km) northward on the Great Plains. They are separated from the Absaroka Range, which lie on the main branch of the Rockies to the west, by the Bighorn Basin. Much of the land is contained within the Bighorn National Forest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Big_Horn_Mountains%2C_Wyoming_02.jpg/330px-Big_Horn_Mountains%2C_Wyoming_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bighorn_Mountains","wikidata":"Q643243"},{"title":"Horn Mountains","blocks":[{"text":"The Horn Mountains, el. 7,638 feet (2,328 m), is a small mountain range northwest of Henry's Lake, Idaho in Madison County, Montana."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horn_Mountains","wikidata":"Q14704561"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Wpdms_nasa_topo_bighorn_mountains.jpg/330px-Wpdms_nasa_topo_bighorn_mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Wpdms_nasa_topo_bighorn_mountains.jpg/330px-Wpdms_nasa_topo_bighorn_mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWpdms_nasa_topo_bighorn_mountains.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/BighornMountainsWyoming.jpg/330px-BighornMountainsWyoming.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/BighornMountainsWyoming.jpg/960px-BighornMountainsWyoming.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/BighornMountainsWyoming.j